# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings, whatever core.autocrlf says.
#
# This is not a tidiness preference. With CRLF, `set -euo pipefail` becomes
# `set -euo pipefail\r` and bash rejects it as "invalid option name", so the
# script dies on line 1. It bit here after a `git reset --hard`, which rewrote
# the working tree through autocrlf: scripts/run_sim.sh still ran under the
# MSYS bash on PATH but failed under the Git bash a Python subprocess picked,
# so the same script passed interactively and failed from a wrapper.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else with a shebang or a strict parser -- but
# SCOPED to this project's own scripts. A bare `*.tcl` also matches sys/*.tcl,
# which is vendored MiSTer framework code; rewriting its line endings would
# diverge from upstream Template_MiSTer for no benefit and make the next sys/
# update a needless conflict.
scripts/**/*.tcl text eol=lf
scripts/**/*.lua text eol=lf

# Verilog/VHDL and docs are read by tools that do not care; leave them to
# core.autocrlf so Windows editors behave normally.
